Z||000000||lbetts||07-11-2000||04:20 AM||||help:: car is a 84 4 cyl. no modifications broke a water pump belt. over heated 3 times getting it to the garage to work on it. am not getting any gas or spark. replaced electronic module in the distributor. by the way forgot to mention that i am getting gas as far as fuel injection system, but none comes out.  so we have no spark and injector not opening.<P>well i found out by removing the oil fill cap and looking in there while the motor was being cranked that there is no movement.  so i guess i will be doing a motor overhaul.  probably internal gears broke or keys or something.   thanks to all you guys that wrote, well i guess i had better dust off the tools, and get busy,  thanks  lorenzo<BR>------------------<P><BR>[This message has been edited by lbetts (edited 07-11-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by lbetts (edited 07-24-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000001||coyote||07-11-2000||10:27 AM||||well lets see you have no gas and no spark. First when you key the car can you hear the fuel pump wind up. If not there is only one thing that controls both the ignition system and the fuel pump and thats the ECM. The first thing i would do is check the ECM fuse in the fuse block then you could pull the armrest and check your connections on the ECM, If i remember correctly the Fuel pump harness is seperated at the firewall, but it would be unlikely that you burned both of them anyhow.||||reg||1
Z||000002||coyote||07-11-2000||10:27 AM||||well lets see you have no gas and no spark. First when you key the car can you hear the fuel pump wind up. If not there is only one thing that controls both the ignition system and the fuel pump and thats the ECM. The first thing i would do is check the ECM fuse in the fuse block then you could pull the armrest and check your connections on the ECM, If i remember correctly the Fuel pump harness is seperated at the firewall, but it would be unlikely that you burned both of them anyhow.<P>Coyote<BR>84 SE<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||lbetts||07-11-2000||11:38 AM||||thanks for responding.  i am getting gas to injector but none comes out, so fuel pump system is good.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by coyote:<BR><B>well lets see you have no gas and no spark. First when you key the car can you hear the fuel pump wind up. If not there is only one thing that controls both the ignition system and the fuel pump and thats the ECM. The first thing i would do is check the ECM fuse in the fuse block then you could pull the armrest and check your connections on the ECM, If i remember correctly the Fuel pump harness is seperated at the firewall, but it would be unlikely that you burned both of them anyhow.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||coyote||07-12-2000||02:07 PM||||If you are not getting gas out of the injector then i would hazard a guess that the ECM is fried, If you have a shop manual i am sure that there are tests that can be perfomed to verify that. you can also trace the harness throough the car and make sure that they are intact. or just use a D,V,M. and go from the car to the coil and injector leads.<P>Coyote<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||07-12-2000||02:19 PM||||<BR>Are you sure that it is simply just an injector problem?<P>Personally, I would not yet consider the ECM fried.  I would replace the injector first, then if the engine still does not run, then you have a bigger problem on your hands.<P>I have an '84 that had similiar problems, and I did a major tuneup (plugs, wires, dist. cap & rotor, pcv valve, and fuel filter) that did not solve my problem. So I replaced the injector and reset the timing, my problems went away.<P>Just my $.02 - Good Luck<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000006||ROB MAC||07-12-2000||02:59 PM||||Suddenly no spark and no injector sounds like no electricity. Check for voltage at the source. Since the belt snapped maybe the alt. is seized or ...||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||07-12-2000||04:38 PM||||No spark. No EFI. It's not an alternator.<P>The car will run w/o an alt. Even w/o a belt but not long before it gets REal hot.<P>I'd check all the fuses up front and fuse links on the starter. The Battery cables as well. The belt can come off hard enough to whack the hell out of things. It could have whacked either the battery cables.<P>Once that's all checked I'd look at the ECM. If the fuel injector OR the module was bad you'd still have fuel spray OR spark. You have neither.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||07-14-2000||01:11 PM||||First thing I would look for is a harness connector at the front that didn't get reconnected. Sounds like the harness that feeds the ignition information to the ECM may not be reconnected, or at least, not all the way. There's no real common denominator between the distributer and fuel injection system electrically, with the exception of the distributer (and the ECM of course) it just seems a little bit unlikely that the two systems would crap at the same time. <BR>Hope this helps some anyway.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000009||lbetts||07-15-2000||09:31 AM||||thanks for your input, i will check this out.<P>thanks again   lbetts<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>First thing I would look for is a harness connector at the front that didn't get reconnected. Sounds like the harness that feeds the ignition information to the ECM may not be reconnected, or at least, not all the way. There's no real common denominator between the distributer and fuel injection system electrically, with the exception of the distributer (and the ECM of course) it just seems a little bit unlikely that the two systems would crap at the same time. <BR>Hope this helps some anyway.<P>-Taiji</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||gmh||01-13-2001||11:52 PM||||I would check the connections to the ECM for loose or dirty connections. That is what happened to me, the ECM would not give up any fuel after replacing many parts, it ended up to be as simple as that, also make sure you have a good battery.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fiero Fanatic||01-14-2001||04:24 AM||||Sounds to me that your timing gear is chewed up. The same exact thing happened to me. My first clue was to look under the oil filler cap. I had no movement with the rocker arms when the engine was cranked over. See the forum / Got Fiero Tips?||||reg||1
